#: ../../../contributing.md:53
msgid "Reporting Upstream Bugs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:55
msgid ""
"If you are aware that a bug is caused by an upstream GMT issue rather than a "
"PyGMT-specific issue, you can optionally take the following steps to help "
"resolve the problem:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:59
msgid ""
"Add the line `pygmt.config(GMT_VERBOSE=\"d\")` after your import statements, "
"which will report the equivalent GMT commands as one of the debug messages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:61
msgid ""
"Either append all messages from running your script to your GitHub issue, or "
"filter the messages to include only the GMT-equivalent commands using a "
"command such as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:65
msgid ""
"python <test>.py 2>&1 | awk -F': ' '$2==\"GMT_Call_Command string\" {print "
"$3}'\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:67
msgid "where `<test>` is the name of your test script."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:68
msgid ""
"If the bug is produced when passing an in-memory data object (e.g., a pandas."
"DataFrame or xarray.DataArray) to a PyGMT function, try writing the data to "
"a file (e.g., a netCDF or ASCII txt file) and passing the data file to the "
"PyGMT function instead. In the GitHub issue, please share the results for "
"both cases along with your code."
msgstr ""

