#: ../../../contributing.md:106
msgid "Getting Help"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:108
msgid ""
"Discussion often happens on GitHub issues and pull requests. In addition, "
"there is a [Discourse forum](https://forum.generic-mapping-tools.org/c/"
"questions/pygmt-q-a) for the project where you can ask questions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:112
msgid "Pull Request Workflow"
msgstr "拉取請求工作流程"

#: ../../../contributing.md:114
msgid ""
"We follow the [git pull request workflow](https://www.asmeurer.com/git-"
"workflow) to make changes to our codebase. Every change made goes through a "
"pull request, even our own, so that our [continuous integration](https://en."
"wikipedia.org/wiki/Continuous_integration) services have a chance to check "
"that the code is up to standards and passes all our tests. This way, the "
"*main* branch is always stable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:121
msgid "General Guidelines for Making a Pull Request (PR):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:123
msgid "What should be included in a PR"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:124
msgid ""
"Have a quick look at the titles of all the existing issues first. If there "
"is already an issue that matches your PR, leave a comment there to let us "
"know what you plan to do. Otherwise, **open an issue** describing what you "
"want to do."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:128
msgid ""
"Each pull request should consist of a **small** and logical collection of "
"changes; larger changes should be broken down into smaller parts and "
"integrated separately."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:131
msgid "Bug fixes should be submitted in separate PRs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:132
msgid "How to write and submit a PR"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:133
msgid ""
"Use underscores, not hyphens, in the names of all files and directories, as "
"per [PEP8](https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0008/) for Python (\\*.py) "
"files. A few exceptions are allowed (e.g., `.pre-commit-config.yaml`) and "
"are listed in the [style checks](https://github.com/GenericMappingTools/"
"pygmt/blob/main/.github/workflows/style_checks.yaml) workflow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:138
msgid ""
"Describe what your PR changes and *why* this is a good thing. Be as specific "
"as you can. The PR description is how we keep track of the changes made to "
"the project over time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:141
msgid ""
"Do not commit changes to files that are irrelevant to your feature or bugfix "
"(e.g.: `.gitignore`, IDE project files, etc)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:143
msgid ""
"Write descriptive commit messages. Chris Beams has written a [guide](https://"
"chris.beams.io/posts/git-commit/) on how to write good commit messages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:146
msgid "PR review"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:147
msgid ""
"Be willing to accept criticism and work on improving your code; we don't "
"want to break other users' code, so care must be taken not to introduce bugs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:150
msgid ""
"Be aware that the pull request review process is not immediate, and is "
"generally proportional to the size of the pull request."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:153
msgid "General Process for Pull Request Review:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:155
msgid ""
"After you've submitted a pull request, you should expect to hear at least a "
"comment within a couple of days. We may suggest some changes, improvements "
"or alternative implementation details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:159
msgid ""
"To increase the chances of getting your pull request accepted quickly, try "
"to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:161
msgid "Submit a friendly PR"
msgstr "提交友善的 PR"

#: ../../../contributing.md:162
msgid "Write a good and detailed description of what the PR does."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:163
msgid ""
"Write some documentation for your code (docstrings) and leave comments "
"explaining the *reason* behind non-obvious things."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:165
msgid ""
"Write tests for the code you wrote/modified if needed. Please refer to "
"[Testing your code](contributing.md#testing-your-code) or [Testing plots]"
"(contributing.md#testing-plots)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:168
msgid ""
"Include an example of new features in the gallery or tutorials. Please refer "
"to [Gallery plots](contributing.md#contributing-gallery-plots) or [Tutorials]"
"(contributing.md#contributing-tutorials). If adding a new method/function/"
"class, the gallery example or tutorial should be submitted in a separate "
"pull request."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:173
msgid "Have a good coding style"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:174
msgid ""
"Use readable code, as it is better than clever code (even with comments)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:175
msgid ""
"Follow the [PEP8](https://pep8.org) style guide for code and the [NumPy "
"style guide](https://numpydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/format.html) for "
"docstrings. Please refer to [Code style](contributing.md#code-style)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:179
msgid ""
"Pull requests will automatically have tests run by GitHub Actions. This "
"includes running both the unit tests as well as code linters. GitHub will "
"show the status of these checks on the pull request. Try to get them all "
"passing (green). If you have any trouble, leave a comment in the PR or [get "
"in touch](contributing.md#getting-help)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:186
msgid "Setting up your Environment"
msgstr "設定您的環境"

#: ../../../contributing.md:188
msgid ""
"These steps for setting up your environment are necessary for [editing the "
"documentation locally](contributing.md#editing-the-documentation-locally) "
"and [contributing code](contributing.md#contributing-code). A local PyGMT "
"development environment is not needed for [editing the documentation on "
"GitHub](contributing.md#editing-the-documentation-on-github)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:193
msgid ""
"We highly recommend using [Miniforge](https://github.com/conda-forge/"
"miniforge#miniforge3) and the `conda` package manager to install and manage "
"your Python packages. It will make your life a lot easier!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:197
msgid ""
"The repository includes a virtual environment file `environment.yml` with "
"the specification for all development requirements to build and test the "
"project. In particular, these are some of the key development dependencies "
"you will need to install to build the documentation and run the unit tests "
"locally:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:202
msgid "git (for cloning the repo and tracking changes in code)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:203
msgid ""
"[Git LFS](https://git-lfs.com) (for downloading baseline images used in "
"tests)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:204
msgid "pytest-mpl (for checking that generated plots match the baseline)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:205
msgid "sphinx-gallery (for building the gallery example page)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:207
msgid ""
"See the [`environment.yml`](https://github.com/GenericMappingTools/pygmt/"
"blob/main/environment.yml) file for the full list of dependencies and the "
"environment name (`pygmt`). Once you have forked and cloned the repository "
"to your local machine, you can use this file to create an isolated "
"environment on which you can work. Run the following on the base of the "
"repository to create a new conda environment from the `environment.yml` file:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:214
msgid "conda env create --file environment.yml\n"
msgstr "conda env create --file environment.yml\n"

#: ../../../contributing.md:218
msgid ""
"Before building and testing the project, you have to activate the "
"environment (you'll need to do this every time you start a new terminal):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:221
msgid "conda activate pygmt\n"
msgstr "conda activate pygmt\n"

#: ../../../contributing.md:225
msgid ""
"We have a [`Makefile`](https://github.com/GenericMappingTools/pygmt/blob/"
"main/Makefile) that provides commands for installing, running the tests and "
"coverage analysis, running linters, etc. If you don't want to use `make`, "
"open the `Makefile` and copy the commands you want to run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:230
msgid "To install the current source code into your testing environment, run:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:232
msgid ""
"make install  # on Linux/macOS\n"
"python -m pip install --no-deps -e .  # on Windows\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:237
msgid ""
"This installs your project in *editable* mode, meaning that changes made to "
"the source code will be available when you import the package (even if "
"you're on a different directory)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:241
msgid "Contributing Documentation"
msgstr "貢獻文件"

#: ../../../contributing.md:243
msgid "PyGMT Documentation Overview"
msgstr "PyGMT 文件概覽"

#: ../../../contributing.md:245
msgid "There are four main components to PyGMT's documentation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:247
msgid ""
"Gallery examples, with source code in Python `*.py` files under the "
"`examples/gallery/` folder."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:249
msgid ""
"Tutorial examples, with source code in Python `*.py` files under the "
"`examples/tutorials/` folder."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:251
msgid ""
"API documentation, with source code in the docstrings in Python `*.py` files "
"under the `pygmt/src/` and `pygmt/datasets/` folders."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:253
msgid ""
"Getting started/developer documentation, with source text in ReST `*.rst` "
"and markdown `*.md` files under the `doc/` folder."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:256
msgid ""
"The documentation is written primarily in [reStructuredText](https://"
"docutils.sourceforge.io/rst.html) and built by [Sphinx](https://www.sphinx-"
"doc.org/). Please refer to {gmt-docs}`reStructuredText Cheatsheet <devdocs/"
"rst-cheatsheet.html>` if you are new to reStructuredText. When contributing "
"documentation, be sure to follow the general guidelines in the [pull request "
"workflow](contributing.md#pull-request-workflow) section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:264
msgid "There are two primary ways to edit the PyGMT documentation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:265
msgid ""
"For simple documentation changes, you can easily [edit the documentation on "
"GitHub](contributing.md#editing-the-documentation-on-github). This only "
"requires you to have a GitHub account."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:268
msgid ""
"For more complicated changes, you can [edit the documentation locally]"
"(contributing.md#editing-the-documentation-locally). In order to build the "
"documentation locally, you first need to [set up your environment]"
"(contributing.md#setting-up-your-environment)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:273
msgid "Editing the Documentation on GitHub"
msgstr "在 GitHub 上編輯文件"

#: ../../../contributing.md:275
msgid ""
"If you're browsing the documentation and notice a typo or something that "
"could be improved, please consider letting us know by [creating an issue]"
"(contributing.md#reporting-a-bug) or (even better) submitting a fix."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:279
msgid ""
"You can submit fixes to the documentation pages completely online without "
"having to download and install anything:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:282
msgid ""
"On each documentation page, there should be an \"Improve This Page\" link at "
"the very top."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:284
msgid ""
"Click on that link to open the respective source file (usually an `.rst` "
"file in the `doc/` folder or a `.py` file in the `examples/` folder) on "
"GitHub for editing online (you'll need a GitHub account)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:287
msgid "Make your desired changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:288
msgid "When you're done, scroll to the bottom of the page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:289
msgid ""
"Fill out the two fields under \"Commit changes\": the first is a short title "
"describing your fixes; the second is a more detailed description of the "
"changes. Try to be as detailed as possible and describe *why* you changed "
"something."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:292
msgid ""
"Choose \"Create a new branch for this commit and start a pull request\" and "
"click on the \"Propose changes\" button to open a pull request."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:294
msgid ""
"The pull request will run the GMT automated tests and make a preview "
"deployment. You can see how your change looks in the PyGMT documentation by "
"clicking the \"Details\" button of the \"docs/readthedocs.org:pygmt-dev\" "
"status check, after the building has finished (usually 10-15 minutes after "
"the pull request was created)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:298
msgid ""
"We'll review your pull request, recommend changes if necessary, and then "
"merge them in if everything is OK."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:300
msgid "Done!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:302
msgid ""
"Alternatively, you can make the changes offline to the files in the `doc` "
"folder or the example scripts. See [editing the documentation locally]"
"(contributing.md#editing-the-documentation-locally) for instructions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:306
msgid "Editing the Documentation Locally"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:308
msgid ""
"For more extensive changes, you can edit the documentation in your cloned "
"repository and build the documentation to preview changes before submitting "
"a pull request. First, follow the [setting up your environment](contributing."
"md#setting-up-your-environment) instructions. After making your changes, you "
"can build the HTML files from sources using:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:313
msgid ""
"cd doc\n"
"make all\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:318
msgid ""
"This will build the HTML files in `doc/_build/html`. Open `doc/_build/html/"
"index.html` in your browser to view the pages. Follow the [pull request "
"workflow](contributing.md#pull-request-workflow) to submit your changes for "
"review."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:322
msgid "Adding example code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:324
msgid ""
"Many of the PyGMT functions have example code in their documentation. To "
"contribute an example, add an \"Example\" header and put the example code "
"below it. Have all lines begin with `>>>`.  To keep this example code from "
"being run during testing, add the code `__doctest_skip__ = "
"[\"function_name\"]` to the top of the module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:329
msgid "**Inline code example**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:331
msgid "Below the import statements at the top of the file:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:333
msgid "__doctest_skip__ = [\"function_name\"]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:337
msgid "At the end of the function's docstring:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:339
msgid ""
"Example\n"
"-------\n"
">>> import pygmt\n"
">>> # Comment describing what is happening\n"
">>> Code example\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:347
msgid "Contributing Gallery Plots"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:349
msgid ""
"The gallery and tutorials are managed by [sphinx-gallery](https://sphinx-"
"gallery.readthedocs.io/). The source files for the example gallery are `.py` "
"scripts in `examples/gallery/` that generate one or more figures. They are "
"executed automatically by sphinx-gallery when the [documentation is built]"
"(contributing.md#editing-the-documentation-locally). The output is gathered "
"and assembled into the gallery."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:356
msgid ""
"You can **add a new** plot by placing a new `.py` file in one of the folders "
"inside the `examples/gallery` folder of the repository. See the other "
"examples to get an idea for the format."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:360
msgid "General guidelines for making a good gallery plot:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:362
msgid ""
"Examples should highlight a single feature/command. Good: *how to add a "
"label to a colorbar*. Bad: *how to add a label to the colorbar and use two "
"different CPTs and use subplots*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:365
msgid ""
"Try to make the example as simple as possible. Good: *use only commands that "
"are required to show the feature you want to highlight*. Bad: *use advanced/"
"complex Python features to make the code smaller*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:368
msgid ""
"Use a sample dataset from `pygmt.datasets` if you need to plot data. If a "
"suitable dataset isn't available, open an issue requesting one and we'll "
"work together to add it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:371
msgid ""
"Add comments to explain things that aren't obvious from reading the code. "
"Good: *Use a Mercator projection and make the plot 15 centimeters wide*. "
"Bad: *Draw coastlines and plot the data*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:374
msgid ""
"Describe the feature that you're showcasing and link to other relevant parts "
"of the documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:376
msgid "SI units should be used in the example code for gallery plots."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:378
msgid "Contributing Tutorials"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:380
msgid ""
"The tutorials (the User Guide in the docs) are also built by sphinx-gallery "
"from the `.py` files in the `examples/tutorials` folder of the repository. "
"To add a new tutorial:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:383
msgid "Create a `.py` file in the `examples/tutorials/advanced` folder."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:384
msgid ""
"Write the tutorial in \"notebook\" style with code mixed with paragraphs "
"explaining what is being done. See the other tutorials for the format."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:386
msgid ""
"Choose the most representative figure as the thumbnail figure by adding the "
"comment line `# sphinx_gallery_thumbnail_number = <fig_number>` at the end "
"of the tutorial. The *fig_number* starts from 1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:390
msgid "Guidelines for a good tutorial:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:392
msgid ""
"Each tutorial should focus on a particular set of tasks that a user might "
"want to accomplish: plotting grids, interpolation, configuring the frame, "
"projections, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:394
msgid ""
"The tutorial code should be as simple as possible. Avoid using advanced/"
"complex Python features or abbreviations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:396
msgid ""
"Explain the options and features in as much detail as possible. The gallery "
"has concise examples while the tutorials are detailed and full of text."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:398
msgid "SI units should be used in the example code for tutorial plots."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:400
msgid ""
"Note that the <code>pygmt.Figure.show</code> method needs to be called for a "
"plot to be inserted into the documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:400 ../../../contributing.md:443
#: ../../../contributing.md:444 ../../../contributing.md:445
#: ../../../contributing.md:446 ../../../contributing.md:448
#: ../../../contributing.md:458 ../../../contributing.md:459
#: ../../../contributing.md:461 ../../../contributing.md:602
msgid "<code>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:400 ../../../contributing.md:443
#: ../../../contributing.md:444 ../../../contributing.md:445
#: ../../../contributing.md:446 ../../../contributing.md:448
#: ../../../contributing.md:458 ../../../contributing.md:459
#: ../../../contributing.md:461 ../../../contributing.md:602
msgid "</code>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:404
msgid "Editing the API Documentation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:406
msgid ""
"The API documentation is built from the docstrings in the Python `*.py` "
"files under the `pygmt/src/` and `pygmt/datasets/` folders. **All "
"docstrings** should follow the [NumPy style guide](https://numpydoc."
"readthedocs.io/en/latest/format.html#docstring-standard). All functions/"
"classes/methods should have docstrings with a full description of all "
"arguments and return values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:412
msgid ""
"While the maximum line length for code is automatically set by Ruff, "
"docstrings must be formatted manually. To play nicely with Jupyter and "
"IPython, **keep docstrings limited to 88 characters** per line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:416
msgid "Standards for Example Code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:418
msgid ""
"When editing documentation, use the following standards to demonstrate the "
"example code:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:420
msgid ""
"Python arguments, such as import statements, Boolean expressions, and "
"function arguments should be wrapped as ``code`` by using \\`\\` on both "
"sides of the code. Examples: \\`\\`import pygmt\\`\\` results in ``import "
"pygmt``, \\`\\`True\\`\\` results in `True`,  \\`\\`style=\"v\"\\`\\` "
"results in `style=\"v\"`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:424
msgid ""
"Literal GMT arguments should be **bold** by wrapping the arguments with "
"\\*\\* (two asterisks) on both sides. The argument description should be in "
"*italicized* with \\* (single asterisk) on both sides. Examples: `**+l**\\ "
"*label*` results in **+l***label*, `**05m**` results in **05m**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:428
msgid "Optional arguments are wrapped with [ ] (square brackets)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:429
msgid ""
"Arguments that are mutually exclusive are separated with a | (bar) to denote "
"\"or\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:430
msgid ""
"Default arguments for parameters and configuration settings are wrapped with "
"[ ] (square brackets) with the prefix \"Default is\". Example: [Default is "
"**p**]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:434
msgid "Cross-referencing with Sphinx"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:436
msgid ""
"The API reference is manually assembled in `doc/api/index.rst`. The "
"*autodoc* sphinx extension will automatically create pages for each function/"
"class/module/method listed there."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:440
msgid ""
"You can reference functions, classes, modules, and methods from anywhere "
"(including docstrings) using:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:443
msgid "<code>:func:\\`package.module.function\\`</code>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:444
msgid "<code>:class:\\`package.module.class\\`</code>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:445
msgid "<code>:meth:\\`package.module.method\\`</code>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:446
msgid "<code>:mod:\\`package.module\\`</code>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:448
msgid ""
"An example would be to use <code>:meth:\\`pygmt.Figure.grdview\\`</code> to "
"link to [https://www.pygmt.org/latest/api/generated/pygmt.Figure.grdview."
"html](https://www.pygmt.org/latest/api/generated/pygmt.Figure.grdview.html). "
"PyGMT documentation that is not a class, method, or module can be linked "
"with <code>:doc:\\`Any Link Text </path/to/the/file>\\`</code>. For example, "
"<code>:doc:\\`Install instructions \\</install\\>\\`</code> links to "
"[https://www.pygmt.org/latest/install.html](https://www.pygmt.org/latest/"
"install.html)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:456
msgid ""
"Linking to the GMT documentation and GMT configuration parameters can be "
"done using:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:458
msgid "<code>:gmt-docs:\\`page_name.html\\`</code>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:459
msgid "<code>:gmt-term:\\`GMT_PARAMETER\\`</code>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:461
msgid ""
"An example would be using <code>:gmt-docs:\\`makecpt.html\\`</code> to link "
"to {gmt-docs}`makecpt.html`. For GMT configuration parameters, an example is "
"<code>:gmt-term:\\`COLOR_FOREGROUND\\`</code> to link to {gmt-term}"
"`COLOR_FOREGROUND`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:466
msgid ""
"Sphinx will create a link to the automatically generated page for that "
"function/class/module/method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:469
msgid "Contributing Code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:471
msgid "PyGMT Code Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:473
msgid ""
"The source code for PyGMT is located in the `pygmt/` directory. When "
"contributing code, please open an issue first to discuss the feature and its "
"implementation and be sure to follow the general guidelines in the [pull "
"request workflow](#pull-request-workflow) section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:478
msgid "Code Style"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:480
msgid ""
"We use the [Ruff](https://docs.astral.sh/ruff) tool to format the code, so "
"we don't have to think about it. It loosely follows the [PEP8](https://pep8."
"org) guide but with a few differences. Regardless, you won't have to worry "
"about formatting the code yourself. Before committing, run it to "
"automatically format your code:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:485
msgid "make format\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:489
msgid ""
"For consistency, we also use `pre-commit` hooks (via [`prek`](https://prek."
"j178.dev/)) to enforce UNIX-style line endings (`\\n`) and file permission "
"644 (`-rw-r--r--`) throughout the whole project. Don't worry if you forget "
"to do it. Our continuous integration systems will warn us and you can make a "
"new commit with the formatted code. Even better, you can just write `/"
"format` in the first line of any comment in a pull request to lint the code "
"automatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:496
msgid ""
"When introducing a new parameter name, use an underscore to separate words. "
"This improves readability and aligns with the [PEP 8 style guide](https://"
"peps.python.org/pep-0008/). For common shortcuts no underscore is needed, e."
"g., `surftype`, `outgrid`, or `timefmt`. This convention is not applied by "
"the code checking tools, but the PyGMT maintainers will comment on any pull "
"requests as needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:502
msgid ""
"When working on a tutorial or a gallery plot, it is good practice to use "
"code block separators to split a long script into multiple blocks. The "
"separators also make it possible to run the script like a Jupyter notebook "
"in some modern text editors or IDEs. We consistently use `# %%` as code "
"block separators (please refer to [issue #2660](https://github.com/"
"GenericMappingTools/pygmt/issues/2660) for the discussions) and require at "
"least one separator in all example files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:509
msgid ""
"We also use [Ruff](https://docs.astral.sh/ruff) to check the quality of the "
"code and quickly catch common errors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:512
msgid ""
"The [`Makefile`](https://github.com/GenericMappingTools/pygmt/blob/main/"
"Makefile) contains rules for running the linter checks:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:515
msgid "make check   # Runs Ruff in check mode\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:519
msgid "Wrapping a GMT Module"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:521
msgid ""
"Wrapping a GMT module in PyGMT is usually a big task, but it can progress "
"more smoothly and efficiently when divided into **small, manageable "
"chunks**. This section gives an overview of the main tasks involved."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:525
msgid ""
"Open \"wrapper request\" issue - create a feature request for wrapping a "
"module and discuss what features should be available in the wrapper "
"[optional, usually done by users]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:528
msgid ""
"Open a \"wrapper tracking\" issue - use the \"Wrapper for a GMT module\" "
"issue template, to track the progress of wrapping the module. Link it to the "
"[Project board](https://github.com/orgs/GenericMappingTools/projects/3), and "
"close the \"wrapper request issue\" with a comment such as [usually done by "
"maintainers]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:532
msgid ""
"Thank you for opening the feature request. The progress of wrapping the "
"module will be tracked in issue #XXX and the [Project board](https://github."
"com/orgs/GenericMappingTools/projects/3)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:535
msgid ""
"Open one PR for the initial implementation, focusing on required and "
"essential parameters [done by maintainers or contributors]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:537
msgid ""
"Close the \"wrapper tracking issue\" once the initial implementation is "
"merged. Leave a comment such as [done by maintainers]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:539
msgid ""
"The initial implementation of wrapping the XXX module was completed in PR "
"#XXX. Not all functionalities are implemented yet. Further progress will be "
"tracked in the Project board.    This is necessary to avoid having too many "
"long-term open issues."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:543
msgid ""
"Open one or more PRs to implement the remaining features and missing aliases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:544
msgid "Open one PR to add a gallery example or a tutorial."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:546
msgid ""
"These PRs can be split among multiple contributors. There is no obligation "
"for a single contributor to complete all steps. Please comment on the "
"\"wrapper tracking issue\" if you would like to open a PR for any of these "
"tasks to avoid redundant efforts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:550
msgid "Initial Feature Implementation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:552
msgid ""
"First, comment on the \"Wrapper tracking issue\" that you will be working on "
"the initial implementation. This first pull request should be as minimal as "
"possible - only adding the required functionality (i.e., wrapping the "
"required GMT parameters and supporting the primary input/output types)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:557
msgid ""
"The following steps are common to all initial implementation pull requests "
"that wrap a GMT module:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:560
msgid ""
"Create a new module `<module-name>.py` in `pygmt/src`. The module docstring "
"should include the module name and a short description of the functionality "
"(e.g., `grdfill - Fill blank areas from a grid.`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:563
msgid ""
"Add a function `<module-name>` to the module. When writing the new function, "
"it is generally easiest to reference the source code for other functions "
"that input/output similar object types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:566
msgid ""
"Write a detailed docstring following the [numpy style guide](https://"
"numpydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/format.html)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:568
msgid ""
"Add the function to the import statements in `pygmt/src/__init__.py` and "
"`pygmt/__init__.py`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:570
msgid ""
"Add the function to appropriate section of the API documentation in `doc/api/"
"index.rst`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:571
msgid ""
"Add a testing module `test_<module-name>.py` in `pygmt/tests`, following the "
"guidelines in the [testing your code](#testing-your-code) section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:574
msgid "Add Missing Aliases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:576
msgid ""
"After the initial implementation, missing aliases can be added in separate "
"PRs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:578
msgid ""
"Select a suitable alias for each GMT option, following the guidelines in the "
"[code style](#code-style) section. Before creating a new alias, check:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:581
msgid ""
"whether the parameter is listed in the `COMMON_PARAMETERS` dictionary in "
"`pygmt/helpers/decorators.py`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:583
msgid "whether other wrapped GMT modules have a similar parameter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:584
msgid ""
"whether [GMT.jl](https://www.generic-mapping-tools.org/GMTjl_doc/) has "
"defined an alias"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:585
msgid "Add the alias to the `AliasSystem` class and the function signature."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:586
msgid ""
"Add the alias and description to the parameters section of the docstring, "
"using the `fmt_docstring` decorator to add descriptions for parameters "
"included in the `COMMON_PARAMETERS` dictionary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:590
msgid "Testing your Code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:592
msgid ""
"Automated testing helps ensure that our code is as free of bugs as it can "
"be. It also lets us know immediately if a change we make breaks any other "
"part of the code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:595
msgid ""
"All of our test code and data are stored in the `tests` subpackage. We use "
"the [pytest](https://pytest.org/) framework to run the test suite."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:598
msgid ""
"Please write tests for your code so that we can be sure that it won't break "
"any of the existing functionality. Tests also help us be confident that we "
"won't break your code in the future."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:602
msgid ""
"When writing tests, don't test everything that the GMT function already "
"tests, such as every unique combination of arguments. An exception to this "
"would be the most popular methods, such as <code>pygmt.Figure.plot</code> "
"and <code>pygmt.Figure.basemap</code>. The highest priority for tests should "
"be the Python-specific code, such as NumPy, pandas, and xarray objects and "
"the virtual file mechanism."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:608
msgid ""
"If you're **new to testing**, see existing test files for examples of things "
"to do. **Don't let the tests keep you from submitting your contribution!** "
"If you're not sure how to do this or are having trouble, submit your pull "
"request anyway. We will help you create the tests and sort out any kind of "
"problem during code review."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:614
msgid ""
"Pull the latest changes (including baseline images), run the tests, and "
"calculate test coverage using:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:617
msgid ""
"git pull\n"
"make test\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:620
msgid ""
"The coverage report will let you know which lines of code are touched by the "
"tests. If all the tests pass, you can view the coverage reports by opening "
"`htmlcov/index.html` in your browser. **Strive to get 100% coverage for the "
"lines you changed.** It's OK if you can't or don't know how to test "
"something. Leave a comment in the PR and we'll help you out."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:626
msgid "You can also run tests in just one test script using:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:628
msgid "pytest pygmt/tests/NAME_OF_TEST_FILE.py\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:630
msgid ""
"or run tests which contain names that match a specific keyword expression:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:632
msgid "pytest -k KEYWORD pygmt/tests\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:634
msgid "Testing Plots"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:636
msgid ""
"Writing an image-based test is only slightly more difficult than a simple "
"test. The main consideration is that you must specify the \"baseline\" or "
"reference image, and compare it with a \"generated\" or test image. This is "
"handled using the *decorator* functions `@pytest.mark.mpl_image_compare` and "
"`@check_figures_equal` whose usage are further described below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:642
msgid "Using mpl_image_compare"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:644
msgid "**This is the preferred way to test plots whenever possible.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:646
msgid ""
"This method uses the [pytest-mpl](https://github.com/matplotlib/pytest-mpl) "
"plug-in to test plot generating code. Every time the tests are run, `pytest-"
"mpl` compares the generated plots with known correct ones stored in `pygmt/"
"tests/baseline`. If your test created a `pygmt.Figure` object, you can test "
"it by adding a *decorator* and returning the `pygmt.Figure` object:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:653
msgid ""
"@pytest.mark.mpl_image_compare\n"
"def test_my_plotting_case():\n"
"    \"\"\"\n"
"    Test that my plotting method works.\n"
"    \"\"\"\n"
"    fig = Figure()\n"
"    fig.basemap(region=[0, 360, -90, 90], projection=\"W15c\", frame=True)\n"
"    return fig\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:664
msgid ""
"Your test function **must** return the `pygmt.Figure` object and you can "
"only test one figure per function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:667
msgid ""
"Before you can run your test, you'll need to generate a *baseline* (a "
"correct version) of your plot. Run the following from the repository root:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:671
msgid "pytest --mpl-generate-path=baseline pygmt/tests/NAME_OF_TEST_FILE.py\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:675
msgid ""
"This will create a `baseline` folder with all the plots generated in your "
"test file. Visually inspect the one corresponding to your test function. If "
"it's correct, copy it (and only it) to `pygmt/tests/baseline`. When you run "
"`make test` the next time, your test should be executed and passing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:682
msgid ""
"Don't forget to commit the baseline image as well! Baseline images are "
"tracked with Git LFS, as explained in the next section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:685
msgid ""
"Using [Git Large File Storage (LFS)](https://git-lfs.com) to Manage Test "
"Images"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:687
msgid ""
"As the baseline images are quite large blob files that can change often (e."
"g., with new GMT versions), they are managed with Git LFS rather than "
"regular Git objects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:690
msgid ""
"You need to run the following command once to set up Git LFS on your machine:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:692
msgid "git lfs install\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:694
msgid ""
"After that, `git clone` and `git pull` download the baseline images "
"automatically. If Git LFS downloads were deliberately skipped, run `git lfs "
"pull` to download the missing images. The images in `pygmt/tests/baseline` "
"can then be used by the image-comparison tests as usual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:699
msgid ""
"The entire workflow for generating or modifying baseline test images can be "
"summarized as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:702
msgid ""
"# Sync Git; Git LFS objects are downloaded automatically\n"
"git pull\n"
"\n"
"# Generate new baseline images\n"
"pytest --mpl-generate-path=baseline pygmt/tests/test_logo.py\n"
"mv baseline/*.png pygmt/tests/baseline/\n"
"\n"
"# Stage the Git LFS-tracked baseline image\n"
"git add pygmt/tests/baseline/test_logo.png\n"
"\n"
"# Commit and push; Git LFS uploads the image automatically\n"
"git commit -m \"Add test_logo.png baseline image\"\n"
"git push\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:716
msgid "Using `check_figures_equal`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:718
msgid ""
"This approach draws the same figure using two different methods (the "
"reference method and the tested method), and checks that both of them are "
"the same. It takes two `pygmt.Figure` objects (`fig_ref` and `fig_test`), "
"generates a png image, and checks for the Root Mean Square (RMS) error "
"between the two. Here's an example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../contributing.md:724
msgid ""
"@check_figures_equal()\n"
"def test_my_plotting_case():\n"
"    \"\"\"\n"
"    Test that my plotting method works.\n"
"    \"\"\"\n"
"    fig_ref, fig_test = Figure(), Figure()\n"
"    fig_ref.grdimage(\"@earth_relief_01d_g\", projection=\"W120/15c\", "
"cmap=\"gmt/geo\")\n"
"    fig_test.grdimage(grid, projection=\"W120/15c\", cmap=\"gmt/geo\")\n"
"    return fig_ref, fig_test\n"
msgstr ""
